<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Time for Exercise</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We Can Swim.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; white-space: nowrap;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Facilitate discussions on classroom topics among peers. Concept: Connect historical events discussed in class to real-life examples.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can lead discussions about classroom topics by asking open-ended questions and prompting peers to elaborate (e.g., "What sports do you like and why?" and gestures to encourage more extended responses). Can link historical events to current events with simple examples (e.g., points to an old picture of a sportsman or sportswoman and says, "He could run very fast; we can too."). Can summarize class discussions, using gestures and visuals (e.g., points to a ball and says, "We all like baseball.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a quick "Can you...?" TPR activity. The teacher says a verb (e.g., "run," "swim," "jump") and students perform the action. The teacher asks, "Can you swim?" (students respond "Yes, I can!" or "No, I cannot!"). This activates prior knowledge of activities and the modal 'can'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models asking about preferences using flashcards of different sports/activities. Teacher points to a picture of swimming and asks, "Do you like swimming?" Then, the teacher models expressing preference: "Yes, I do. I like swimming." The teacher also models encouraging phrases like, "You can do it! Let's swim together!" The teacher clearly pronounces /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "swim," segmenting "wellness" as "well-ness."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I can fly,' is it true or false?" (Expected answer: "False!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I like football, do I say 'I like football' or 'I not like football'?" (Expected answer: "I like football!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___ (5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Time for Exercise"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, illustrated digital presentation or printed poster showcasing various physical activities and sports (e.g., swimming, running, cycling, dancing, football). It includes simple descriptions using target grammar. One slide features an old black-and-white picture of a famous runner or swimmer (e.g., Jesse Owens, Gertrude Ederle) with the sentence, "He/She could run/swim very fast!" next to a modern image of children running/swimming with the caption "We can run/swim fast too!" (genre: informative slides, register: simple, direct).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understand and interpret simple visual and textual information about physical activities and express preferences for them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Teacher asks, "What is this presentation about?" (Expected: "Exercise/Sports"). "What activities do you see?" (Expected: "Swimming, running, etc.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Teacher states sentences based on the presentation (e.g., "The girl likes dancing." or "He could run slowly."), and students indicate true or false by thumbs up/down.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___ (8-10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice asking and expressing preferences for sports/activities, using 'can/cannot', present simple questions, WH-questions with -ing, and encouraging peers. (Grammar: Modal verbs 'can/cannot', Present simple questions/affirmative, WH-question + answer + subject + verb + ing. Vocabulary: walk, exercise, sport, activity, football, swimming, running, hiking, dancing, cycling, play, run, dance, healthy, active, fun, every day, always, sometimes. Pragmatic: Asking/expressing preferences. Sociolinguistic: Encouraging others. Pronunciation: /k/, /s/, segmenting.)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence Builder:</b> Students complete sentences with `can/cannot` and vocabulary words (e.g., "I ____ (swim / bike).", "A fish ____ (walk / swim).", "I like ______ (run / running)."). Teacher checks answers aloud, focusing on pronunciation of /k/ and /s/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Ask & Tell Frames:</b> In pairs, students use sentence frames to ask and answer: "Do you like to ______?" "Yes, I do. I like _______ing every day." or "No, I don't. I like _______ing." "What type of exercise do you like?" "I like _______ing." Teacher prompts students to use adverbs like 'always' or 'sometimes'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Encourage a Friend:</b> Pairs then practice encouraging each other: "Let's ____ together!" or "You can do it! You can ______!" Teacher circulates, providing feedback on accuracy and encouraging use of gestures.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___ (12-15 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Time for Exercise"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: Group Project Presentation and Discussion (21st Century Skills Project Focus: Exercise Plan Poster):</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each student brings their "Exercise Plan Poster" (created in previous lessons) which shows different physical activities and sports they can do. Within their group, each student takes turns presenting their poster. As part of their presentation, they must:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Explain their poster:</b> "On my poster, I have swimming, running, and cycling. I can swim every day." (using modals and present simple).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Facilitate discussion:</b> Ask open-ended questions to their peers about their poster, "What sports do you like on my poster and why?" and prompt them to elaborate using gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Encourage peers:</b> Suggest group activities or motivate classmates, "You can try cycling! It's fun!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connect to past/present:</b> The teacher provides a few simple pictures of old sports heroes. Students can point to one and say, "He could jump very high! We can jump too!" (linking historical example to current abilities).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Summarize:</b> After everyone in the group has presented, one student summarizes the group's preferred activities, "In our group, we all like swimming and dancing!" (using gestures and visuals from their posters).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher circulates, listening, prompting, and providing support for mediation strategies.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___ (10-12 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment:</b> Teacher uses an observation checklist to assess students' mediation skills during the group presentation and discussion. The focus is on their ability to facilitate discussion, connect ideas, summarize, and use target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student leads a short discussion segment (approx. 1-2 minutes) about their "Exercise Plan Poster" within their small group, demonstrating their ability to:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ask a peer an open-ended question about the poster (e.g., "What sport do you like most on my poster and why?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Respond to a peer's comment and encourage them (e.g., "That's a good idea! You can try it!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarize a key idea from the group's discussion about the posters.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempt to link a simple historical sports fact to a current ability (e.g., "She could swim fast. We can swim fast too!").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "swim".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of 'can/cannot' and present simple questions/affirmative sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (sports, activities, adjectives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to ask open-ended questions to facilitate discussion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to encourage peers using simple phrases and gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to summarize discussions, using gestures and visuals.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small paper slip or verbally:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I help my friends understand my exercise poster today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new sport or activity I want to try after talking with my friends?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___ (8-10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and connection</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one thing I did today to help my friends talk about their exercise plans?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one healthy activity I can do every day?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in promoting student mediation and discussion around their exercise posters? (e.g., specific prompts, group dynamics, use of visuals).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for future mediation activities at this level? (e.g., more sentence starters for summarizing, explicit modeling for connecting historical events).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This is the final lesson of this unit. The skills of discussing, summarizing, and encouraging peers will be valuable for future collaborative projects and communication tasks in subsequent themes.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___ (2-3 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>